WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:10.500
For as long as I can remember,
I have loved election day.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:15.400
When I was a kid, I used to follow
my parents into the voting booth,

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.619
pushing each little red
button with intense focus.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.010
After we got home, my parents and
I would sit in the kitchen and

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:27.160
talk about how their small decisions,
and those little buttons,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.500
linked to big decisions, happening
at the highest levels of government.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.902
On my tenth birthday, my grandfather
gave me a blank index card and

00:00:36.902 --> 00:00:40.958
told me to write the top five things
that I wanted to be when I grew up.

00:00:40.958 --> 00:00:46.900
Next to teacher and ballerina, I wrote
that I wanted to work at the White House.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.150
Now, maybe this is because I grew up
in a family that watched the West Wing

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:50.730
every week.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.400
But I like to think that it's because of
all of those times that I'd followed my

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.790
parents into the voting booth.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.410
My grandfather took that index card,
dated it, and

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.590
sealed it in an envelope,
which I promptly forgot about.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:10.820
But I found that envelope stuffed in
the back of a bookshelf 14 years later,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.760
one month after being offered
a job at the White House.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.970
Now, anyone who knows me could
tell you that my childhood

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.810
love of government has only grown.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.000
And to this day, I do still love to vote.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:27.270
But most people don't share my enthusiam.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:33.572
In fact, voter turnout in US midterm
elections tends to hover just below 40%.

00:01:33.572 --> 00:01:38.827
If someone can win with a plurality of
the votes, that means that they can

00:01:38.827 --> 00:01:44.614
get elected to lead with the support
of less than 20% of their constituents.

00:01:46.252 --> 00:01:49.200
Now, you may be asking,
why does this matter for me?

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.360
I want you to take a moment and
think of the products and

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.360
services that you use every day.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.590
Who provides them?

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.440
Raise your hand if you're thinking
of a big tech company right now.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.026
Okay, most of us.

00:02:05.026 --> 00:02:07.080
Now raise your hand if you're
thinking of government.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.800
One or two, but we've got some work to do.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.920
I've had a lot of conversations
with my peers here at

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.060
business school about
the role of government.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.340
And many people have expressed that
they don't see any connection between

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:25.809
the actions they take and
the outcomes that government provides.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:30.430
But in reality, the work of government
touches each of our lives in

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:33.600
countless ways that go beyond
the headlines, every single day.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.110
Let's take a look at your day today.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.150
Your alarm goes off,
you look at the clock and get out of bed.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.359
You check your favorite weather app out
and maybe take a morning medication.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.880
Let's say that you scroll through
notifications on iPhone while eating your

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.310
breakfast and decide to Google a
particularly interesting news story before

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.340
moving on with your day.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.250
Let's recap what just happened.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.830
Who set the time on your alarm clock?

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.690
The National Institute of Standards and
Technology, a US government agency.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:13.690
Your weather app is pulling data
from the National Weather Service.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:17.660
And that medication, why did you
assume that it's safe and effective?

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.670
Because consciously you're not,
you have faith in the work of the Food and

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.590
Drug Administration.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.660
As for the iPhone,

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.390
do you know who provided the early
stage financing for Apple?

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.050
The US Government Small Business
Investment Company Program.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.880
Venture capitalists only
started to take note after

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.190
government funding got Apple
to a proof of concept.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.946
And as for that Google search
of the morning headline,

00:03:44.946 --> 00:03:49.236
It was possible in no small part because
of the National Science Foundation,

00:03:49.236 --> 00:03:52.215
which funded the early work
on Google's algorithm.

00:03:52.215 --> 00:03:59.176
A lot of people here in Silicon Valley see
government as red tape and regulations.

00:03:59.176 --> 00:04:03.564
But that overlooks the massive role that
government has played in spurring some of

00:04:03.564 --> 00:04:05.479
our most disruptive innovations.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:10.220
In many ways, the work of
government is the invisible thread

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.090
holding the fabric of society together.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:17.150
We haven't even left your house yet
in the example that I just walked through.

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:21.910
And there are millions of lives even
more intimately touched by government

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:26.668
every day through things like
unemployment insurance, health care, and

00:04:26.668 --> 00:04:27.966
public education.

00:04:27.966 --> 00:04:32.359
But while its work touches each of our
life every day, whether we know it or not,

00:04:32.359 --> 00:04:35.520
too many of us remain uninterested
in the public sector.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:41.030
Now I recognize that
government is not perfect.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:44.530
There are many improvements to be
made in terms of efficiencies and

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:47.871
user experience,
let alone tangible policy outcomes.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.950
But in the same way that public
companies need shareholders to

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:56.520
provide accountability and counsel,
government needs shareholders too.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:01.460
And that comes in the form
of active civic engagement.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.660
There are countless ways to get involved,
but

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.220
the one that tends to come
up the most is voting.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.750
Now, for any of you who may still be
on the fence about the value of voting,

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:16.760
I could give you an impassioned
plea about how it's your duty,

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.560
given those who fought for
your right to enter the voting booth.

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:24.620
I could tell you about the importance
of having a representative sample

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:25.964
of the population vote,

00:05:25.964 --> 00:05:30.452
so that our elected officials are actually
representative of the general public.

00:05:30.452 --> 00:05:34.718
And for the more data driven members of
the audience, I could tell you that if you

00:05:34.718 --> 00:05:38.920
are in a swing state, the chance of your
one vote changing the election outcome

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:42.760
is actually 18 times better than
your chance of winning the lottery.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.550
But beyond emotional and
data driven appeals,

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.480
I would propose that there are two main
reasons why it's crucial that you vote.

00:05:53.462 --> 00:05:57.580
First, oftentimes there
are many things to vote for

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:03.140
on a ballot beyond just people,
especially here in California.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.071
In this November's midterms,

00:06:06.071 --> 00:06:09.522
there were 11 different statewide
propositions on California ballots.

00:06:09.522 --> 00:06:13.545
Soliciting constituent input on everything
from affordable housing to whether or

00:06:13.545 --> 00:06:16.250
not California should abandon
Daylight Savings Time.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:22.120
And these measures often do pass or
fail on very thin margins.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:27.090
Second, politicians look at who votes.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.186
Whether or not you vote is a public
record here in the United States.

00:06:32.186 --> 00:06:35.370
And that means that, yes,
your neighbors can know whether or

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.370
not you voted in the most
recent elections.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.764
But more importantly, it means that
politicians know who voted and

00:06:42.764 --> 00:06:43.602
who did not.

00:06:43.602 --> 00:06:47.845
And especially at the local level,
when those same politicians are making

00:06:47.845 --> 00:06:50.822
decisions about different
policies down the line.

00:06:50.822 --> 00:06:55.422
For better for worse, they look at who
voted last time as the projection for

00:06:55.422 --> 00:06:57.480
who's likely to vote next time.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:03.787
That means that if you want to be sure
that your interests are heard in how your

00:07:03.787 --> 00:07:08.583
government operates, you have to take on
the mantle of being an active citizen.

00:07:10.619 --> 00:07:13.007
Now, for those of you who already vote,

00:07:13.007 --> 00:07:16.890
government doesn't only
need you on election day.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:20.730
There's a whole spectrum of ways to
get involved the rest of the year.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:24.930
From calling your elected official and
expressing support or concern for an issue

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:29.000
to volunteering for local or national
campaign that you're excited about.

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:31.200
Or getting involved in political tech and

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:34.210
creating a digital solution to help
government better serve the public.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.250
Maybe you help fund
public/private partnerships

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.150
to drive toward meaningful social change.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:44.885
Or maybe you even decide to run for
office, or

00:07:44.885 --> 00:07:49.464
otherwise do a tour of duty of
sorts in government yourself.

00:07:49.464 --> 00:07:53.363
Today, roles in government are no
longer limited to policy adviser,

00:07:53.363 --> 00:07:56.280
project coordinator, and
legislative director.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.060
Today, roles in government include
becoming a presidential innovation fellow,

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:07.290
a user experience designer, or even chief
technology officer of the United States.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:12.860
These are all real roles that
real people hold in government.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.525
And all of them have been held by someone
who decided to leave Silicon Valley for

00:08:17.525 --> 00:08:19.927
a few years and contribute their time and

00:08:19.927 --> 00:08:22.701
talents to the change
that they wanted to see.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.040
Toward the end of my
time at the White House,

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.057
I found myself standing outside the
Oval Office waiting to begin my farewell

00:08:31.057 --> 00:08:33.584
meeting each staffer
gets with the president.

00:08:33.584 --> 00:08:37.565
I was trying to remember to breathe
as I nervously counted the stars and

00:08:37.565 --> 00:08:41.560
stripes on the lapel pin of
the Secret Service officer across from me.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:47.800
Before I knew it, a staffer said next,
and ushered me into the Oval Office,

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:50.930
where Barack Obama was standing
in front of the Resolute desk.

00:08:52.805 --> 00:08:56.200
Now, I had played this moment over and
over in my mind.

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:58.660
I would thank him and

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:03.748
explain what an honor it was to contribute
in even a small way to his legacy.

00:09:03.748 --> 00:09:05.670
And he would thank me and
congratulate me for my work.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.185
But what I remember most
from our conversation that

00:09:12.185 --> 00:09:16.414
day is what President Obama
said at the end.

00:09:16.414 --> 00:09:19.107
We shook hands to say goodbye and

00:09:19.107 --> 00:09:23.209
he paused and
said there's more work to be done.

00:09:24.828 --> 00:09:29.482
Not thank you for what you have done and
not congratulations, but

00:09:29.482 --> 00:09:31.690
there's more work to be done.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.670
There I was expecting to celebrate
the team's accomplishments to date,

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:40.590
only to have the leader of
the free world remind me

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:43.350
of the never ending list of problems yet
to be solved.

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:46.990
And that is a huge challenge.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:54.318
But it's also a huge opportunity, and
one in which all of us can play a part.

00:09:56.258 --> 00:09:59.098
You may have heard John F Kennedy's quote,

00:09:59.098 --> 00:10:04.107
ask not what your country can do for you,
ask what you can do for your country.

00:10:04.107 --> 00:10:06.502
But I don't think that's quite right.

00:10:06.502 --> 00:10:11.220
Yes, you should ask what you can do for
your country.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:15.040
But you should also never stop asking
what your country can do for you.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:20.890
Because if we start thinking about that,
if we start expecting more and

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.480
seeing ourselves as
shareholders in government,

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:29.060
then we can truly bring our government
to its fullest potential together.

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:33.973
Because in the end,
there's always more work to be done.

00:10:33.973 --> 00:10:35.416
Thank you.

00:10:35.416 --> 00:10:36.928
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:36.928 --> 00:10:41.636
[MUSIC]

